#!RuleTest
#test CDISC-CG0148 expect=violation domain=DM
#note "Four probes for the D100/SPEC 5.2 retyping (phase 3c, RUNBOOK-phase3c-corpus-rewrite.md 6.2). Subject P1 is the positive: a genuine day-precision mismatch against the earliest EXSTDTC. P2 and P3 FIRE under plain string equality and are SILENT under typed date equality -- P2 precision (day vs datetime of the same day), P3 UTC offset (same instant spelled with and without an offset). P4 is the UNPOSITIONABLE probe (calendar-impossible day 99 in RFXSTDTC): it was silent from the phase-3c rewrite until H1b and FIRES AGAIN since -- owner ruling 2026-09-17, SPEC 5.2(4) amended (<meta>/plans/SPEC-typed-expression-engine.md): an unbounded hull answers `negate`, so date_not_equal_to fires on an unpositionable operand in EITHER position. That is the right answer here: 2026-02-99 is not a date at all, so it is certainly not the earliest EXSTDTC 2026-01-01, and the rule now says so. So this scenario expects TWO violations, P1 and P4. This scenario is red on the pre-rewrite corpus by construction."
#expectViolationCount 2
#expectViolationAt row=1 USUBJID=P1
#expectViolationAt row=4 USUBJID=P4
dataset DM label=Demographics
col STUDYID type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col RFXSTDTC type=Char label="Date/Time of First Study Treatment"
---
S1 | DM | P1 | 2026-03-15
S1 | DM | P2 | 2026-01-01
S1 | DM | P3 | 2026-01-01T11:00+02:00
S1 | DM | P4 | 2026-02-99
---

dataset EX label=Exposure
col STUDYID type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col EXSTDTC type=Char label="Start Date/Time of Treatment"
col EXENDTC type=Char label="End Date/Time of Treatment"
---
S1 | EX | P1 | 2026-01-01 | 2026-01-10
S1 | EX | P2 | 2026-01-01T09:00 | 2026-01-10
S1 | EX | P3 | 2026-01-01T09:00 | 2026-01-10
S1 | EX | P4 | 2026-01-01 | 2026-01-10
---
